latest_plan = "Based on the revised understanding, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialize:\n   - Create a new grid (new_grid) of the same dimensions as the input grid, filled with zeros.\n   - Get the dimensions of the grid: rows and cols.\n\n2. Scan and Compact:\n   - For each column, starting from the rightmost column (col = cols - 1) and moving left:\n     - For each row, from top to bottom:\n       - If the current cell in the input grid is non-zero:\n         - Find the rightmost available position in the new_grid for this element:\n           - Start from the current column in new_grid\n           - Move left until finding a zero or reaching the left edge\n         - Place the element in this rightmost available position in new_grid\n\n3. Return:\n   - Create and return a new ColoredGrid object with the values from new_grid\n\nHere's a more detailed algorithm:\n\n```\nfunction solve_5ffb2104(input_grid):\n    rows, cols = input_grid.get_dimensions()\n    new_grid = [[0 for _ in range(cols)] for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    for col in range(cols - 1, -1, -1):  # Start from rightmost column\n        for row in range(rows):\n            if input_grid.values[row][col] != 0:\n                # Find rightmost available position\n                new_col = col\n                while new_col < cols - 1 and new_grid[row][new_col + 1] == 0:\n                    new_col += 1\n                \n                # Place the element\n                new_grid[row][new_col] = input_grid.values[row][col]\n\n    return ColoredGrid(values=new_grid)\n```\n\nThis algorithm ensures that:\n\n1. Non-zero elements are moved as far right as possible.\n2. The vertical order of elements is preserved (we process each column top to bottom).\n3. The relative horizontal order of elements in each row is maintained (we process columns right to left).\n4. Elements are compacted together without leaving empty spaces between them.\n\nThis solution should work for all the given examples and test cases, as it captures the essence of the transformation: compacting all non-zero elements to the right side of the grid while preserving their relative positions."
